Z||000000||bHooper||08-30-2000||07:32 AM||||just curious, has anyone ever used the springs from the JC Whitney catalog?  I believe they are "suspenion techniques" lowering springs.  Only $79/pair.<P>Are these cut springs, or what?<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||10
Z||000001||2birds||08-30-2000||05:31 PM||||I have them.  They lower it about 1.5".  They are from Suspension Techniques, linear rate, and are very harsh when combined with poly bushings.  I like the handling, but the ride is a kidney buster.<BR>I'd trade in a heartbeat for some variable rate Eibach's, even though they don't lower it as much.<BR>Check out RobertVol's North Florida Fieros site for a pic to see the new ride height.  Mine's the red and silver 86 GT.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||08-30-2000||06:38 PM||||whats the link... i cant seem to find it anywhere<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||08-30-2000||09:51 PM||||I had the Suspension Techniques springs from JCWhiney on an 87 GT with tiny 195/50/15 tires. You sure can't beat the price. I think they are just stock springs that have been cut and repainted. I remember seeing a nick in the coil where it looked like the saw blade hit after cutting through. I didn't have any complaints about ride or handling, and neither does the new owner. Anyway, here is a picture of the car before I sold it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/87gt_1.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||10
Z||000004||rockcrawl||08-30-2000||09:54 PM||||Oh yeah, the rocker panels scraped on nearly every speed bump, no matter how slow I drove, especially when I had a passenger.||||reg||1
