Z||000006||Standard||08-31-2000||02:08 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">is that a corvette emblem on the nose of your gt?? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>And where'd you get that hood?  It looks great!<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">   <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||11
Z||000007||Haze_Performance||08-31-2000||03:02 AM||||I Dont think that Suspension Techniques Springs are just stock cut & repainted. There would be some resale problems with GM if thats all they did. Just because JC Whitney sells them (A well known junk store, Meaning that 80% of there stuff is No-Name products) people think that most of the things sold is low quality. Suspension Techniques, Intrax, Neuspeed and Eibach are all good springs. It just depends on now much money you have to spend. Progressive always being alittle more than Specific Rate. Any car with Poly-everything will ride alittle hard. Then add lowering springs and long trips are hard on the a$$. So in short ( Long Hee-Hee) Go ahead and get the Suspension Techniques, its very afordable at $180.00. and youll be happy at the way the car handles.<P><BR>But thats just my 2 1/2 cents  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good Luck<P> <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycarsig.jpg">  <B>My 1985 2M4 Sport Coupe </B><P><BR><B>My Website's  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/WS6_Fiero.com" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero</A> &                  <A HREF="http://www.drive.to/HMP" TARGET=_blank>H.M.P. </A></B>||||reg||7
Z||000008||bHooper||08-31-2000||07:07 AM||||2birds,  thats the exact car i have... like the look!  was there any problem getting a floor jack under hte car?<P>rockcrawl, is it possible that the majority of the bottoming problem, on your car was caused be the addition of smaller diamter tires?<P>Haze_, so do you think these springs are cut?  arent cut springs less efficient than non cut?  what is the difference between progressive, and not?<P>thanks <P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||08-31-2000||12:30 PM||||Rock, it looks like more than 1.5", As Hooper said was it  the tires? I'm going to drop the little black toy SOON!!!! I don't need any scraps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  But that looks nice!!!<BR>How much did the springs lower it???<P>DXR_DAD||||reg||5
Z||000010||Pontiaddict||08-31-2000||04:51 PM||||Hoop, the difference between specific rate springs and progressive rate springs are that the first couple of inches of travel are softer than the rest. It's so the car travels over small bumps and rough terrain without rattleing the whole car as much. It also has better handliing due to things like that not upseting the suspension as much.||||reg||1
Z||000011||2birds||08-31-2000||06:30 PM||||Thanks for posting the link, BroCar.<BR>I run 225/60/15 in back, and 215/60/15 in front, so I don't scrape, except on parking lot curbs if I'm not careful and pull up too far.  When the tires were new, the fronts would rub slightly at full lock, but how often does that happen, anyway.  Stock 205's didn't rub.  I do bottom out in front easily.  I like the way the tires fill the whel opening now.<BR>As far as a jack, I have 2" high ramps made from plywood and 2X4's to drive up on, then my floor jack will fit under either front or rear crossmember.  Getting it on the alignment rack is a real exercise in patience, and requires watching the guy driving it on like a hawk, so he doesn't trash the rockers or floor pan.<BR>I agree, for the price and the height, they're a deal, but I can get a set of Eibach's from shox.com for about $230.  I'd spend the extra $$$ next time.  Not quite as low, but a little easier ride.  Haze is right on about poly bushings, though.  I had the stock springs in for a while with the poly, and it wasn't much diferent.<BR>I doubt they are cut stock springs.  Mine have the same cut mark, probably from the tool that hold the wire while it is being bent.||||reg||1
